 "drivingQuestion": "What is the ris, and when is a cataract a blemish?",
 "outcome": "not_dialectical",
 "outcomeNote": "Glosses and a supporting baraita on the mishna",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bekhorot.38b.1",
   "daf": "38b",
   "sentenceId": 0,
   "he": "גְּמָ׳ מַאי הָרִיס? אָמַר רַב פָּפָּא: תּוֹרָא בָּרָא דְּעֵינָא. דַּק תְּבַלּוּל, תָּנוּ רַבָּנַן: דַּק מְשׁוּקָּע — הֲרֵי זֶה מוּם, צָף — אֵינוֹ מוּם.",
   "en": "«GEMARA:» The mishna’s list of blemishes of the eye included an eyelid [ris] that was pierced, damaged, or split. The Gemara asks: «What» is the meaning of: «The ris? Rav Pappa says:» It means «the outer line,» or barrier, «of the eye,» i.e., the eyelid. The mishna further stated: «A cataract, a tevallul.» With regard to a cataract, «the Sages taught: A sunken cataract,» i.e., one that is in the eye itself, «is a blemish,» but «a floating» cataract, i.e., one that is floating above the eye, «is not a blemish.»"
  }
 ],
